WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.860
Today I'm talking about
exploratory spatial data

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.360
analysis.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.560
To start, let's
talk about exploratory data

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.150
analysis.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.900
I'm sure most of you
are familiar with it,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:16.740
but good to recap.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.950
So exploratory data
analysis or EDA

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.500
is an approach slash philosophy
for data analysis

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.330
that employs a variety
of techniques,

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.990
mostly graphical to, one, maximize
insight into a data set,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.630
cover underlying structure,
extract variables,

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.490
detect outliers and anomalies.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.770
So one paper, for
example, described

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:41.150
EDA tend to discover
potentially explicable patterns.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.900
So EDA, as we might know, has an
emphasis on data visualization,

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.630
a use of descriptive
statistics, and it really

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.570
emphasizes on discovering
outliers in your data set.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:55.550
So here are two wonderful
graphics from the Tidy Tuesday.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:59.060
This is how I'm guessing most
of us are familiar with EDA

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.080
itself.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.099
So exploratory
spatial data analysis

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:08.265
takes these same concepts and
applies to the spatial data.

00:01:08.265 --> 00:01:09.890
So to understand
that, we have to start

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.432
with the first law of geography,
which states that everything

00:01:12.432 --> 00:01:14.660
is related to everything
else, but near things

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.490
are more related
than distant things.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:23.560
So in essence, ESDA extends
exploratory data analysis

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.060
to spatial relationships.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.710
It asks questions
like, are things

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:28.620
randomly distributed in space?

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.640
Are there spatial outliers?

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.470
Are close things more
similar than things

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.820
that are further away?

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.050
So where EDA compares
a part to the whole, ESDA

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.390
compares a part to
its neighboring parts.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.570
So in ESDA or exploratory
spatial data analysis

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.410
where evaluate a location
relative to its neighboring

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.490
location, not the
whole or group.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.750
Right so I learned exploratory
spatial data analysis

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.920
in grad school, and I learned
it through Python and libraries

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.790
like geopandas, shapely and
pysal. But it was Python

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.580
and I really wanted
to use rstats.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.380
So around 2021, I wanted
to get back into this

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.750
and I discovered the
R package spdep.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.390
So it's the package for
spatial dependence, weighting

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.950
and statistics.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.850
So some things about spdep, it
was first released in 2002

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.160
and it was initially designed
for sp objects, which

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.580
is like the first way to
interact with spatial data in R.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.800
I'm not sure if any of you
have used, it interesting.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.110
And it covers most of
the spatial econometrics.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.490
So statistics, weights,
neighbors and things like that.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:40.620
But in 2002, I was
6 and I essentially

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.120
grew up with the tidyverse.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.120
So when I use spdep,
I have this a little bit

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.443
of a perceived
paradigm gap where

00:02:47.443 --> 00:02:49.860
the way that spdep functions
is different than the way I

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.360
think about R.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.870
So naturally, when I
couldn't make a neighbor

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.780
list in a mutate function
call, I created an issue.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.950
And unfortunately that
issue went nowhere.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:04.080
So my response was to create
a tidy interface called sfdep.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.240
So sfdep is an
interface and extension

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.810
to spdep for
spatial dependence.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.180
And some principles
are that I always want

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.700
to use SF objects for geometry.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:13.950
That's what I'm familiar with,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.460
and most people might be. Always
return dplyr-friendly objects.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.020
So lists, data frames,
vectors and then, additionally,

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.450
functionality shouldn't
be dependent upon dplyr

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.910
for those who don't want dplyr.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.100
And then additionally,
all functionality

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.930
that I've, additional
functionality,

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.240
is implemented
using spdep object

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.820
for backwards compatibility.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.150
And then so fundamentally
with exploratory spatial data

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.580
analysis, we need to
understand our neighbors.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.430
And to do that, we will use the
st contiguity function which

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.540
takes an SF C-class object or
the geometry column of an SF

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.320
object and returns a list for
identifying your neighbors.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.070
Here's an example of that
using dplyr-friendly code.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.950
And the result is a list
with where each element is

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.200
an integer vector, and
the elements contain

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.313
row positions of its neighbors.

00:04:03.313 --> 00:04:05.480
And we're not just limited
to identifying contiguity

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.360
for polygons.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.110
We can implement finding
K-nearest neighbors, distance

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.502
bands, so on and so forth.

00:04:10.502 --> 00:04:12.710
And additionally, if you
want to use spatial weights,

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.960
we just pass in
the neighbor object

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.297
the spatial weights and we get
row standardized by default.

00:04:18.297 --> 00:04:19.880
So here's an example
of doing all that

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.640
in one call with mutate.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.890
Again, we're not
just limited to that.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.150
So let's compare the syntax.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.180
So here's a map of
comparing clusters of crime

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.070
in 1830s France.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.160
Here's the code
for using spdep

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.380
was no pipe operator
and lots of assignments.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.490
Here's the same result using
sfdep. One assignment and two

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.780
pipe operators.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.410
So here's the same map, same
different code, same results.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.890
So sfdep doesn't just
act as an interface,

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.120
it extends it as well.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.165
So we can do local join counts.

00:04:53.165 --> 00:04:55.290
The first open source
implementation of co-location

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:58.710
quotients. It also
implements ESRI's

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.980
Emerging Hot Spot Analysis.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.440
It also integrates
with sfnetworks.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:04.530
So what's next?

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.810
User validation.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.070
Ensure parity with Python and
then make a hex logo one day.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.880
So give it a star
and give it a go.

